1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:04,193
You ate the last fish three days ago.

2
00:00:04,448 --> 00:00:08,442
You scrape something white off the wall of your tunnel and hold it up to the fire.

3
00:00:08,756 --> 00:00:09,618
It's soft.

4
00:00:09,638 --> 00:00:11,936
It smells like the forest after rain.

5
00:00:12,528 --> 00:00:13,794
Pip sniffs it.

6
00:00:14,189 --> 00:00:15,535
And he's watching you.

7
00:00:16,128 --> 00:00:19,203
Nine days ago, you had seven fish.

8
00:00:20,353 --> 00:00:21,838
You have no food left.

9
00:00:21,875 --> 00:00:23,382
And nobody is coming.

10
00:00:23,698 --> 00:00:27,051
At least, not for 66 million years.

11
00:00:30,501 --> 00:00:32,420
Forty days since the impact.

12
00:00:32,441 --> 00:00:34,518
Fifty since you woke up in this world.

13
00:00:34,774 --> 00:00:37,919
Seven fish lie on the shelf you cut into the clay.

14
00:00:38,117 --> 00:00:38,875
One a day.

15
00:00:38,896 --> 00:00:39,990
You've done the math.

16
00:00:40,010 --> 00:00:41,042
Seven days.

17
00:00:41,439 --> 00:00:44,607
Outside, the sky is the colour of wet ash.

18
00:00:45,095 --> 00:00:47,184
Some mornings, the ash is snow.

19
00:00:47,685 --> 00:00:50,195
You remember the first weeks after the impact.

20
00:00:50,216 --> 00:00:52,896
A few dinosaurs made it through the fire too.

21
00:00:53,385 --> 00:00:55,381
One of them was a duck-bill.

22
00:00:55,695 --> 00:00:57,970
So thin you could count its ribs.

23
00:00:58,459 --> 00:01:01,963
It stood by the river every day, chewing on burnt branches.

24
00:01:02,453 --> 00:01:05,842
One morning, you found the last green fern in a wet hollow.

25
00:01:06,435 --> 00:01:11,682
You carried it to the river, broke your own rule, and walked right up to a dinosaur.

26
00:01:12,170 --> 00:01:13,493
Close enough to touch.

27
00:01:13,981 --> 00:01:16,163
You held the fern out.

28
00:01:16,612 --> 00:01:17,766
It looked at you.

29
00:01:17,786 --> 00:01:19,271
It smelled the fern.

30
00:01:19,433 --> 00:01:21,767
It was too weak to lift its head to it.

31
00:01:22,045 --> 00:01:24,530
The next morning, it was lying in the mud.

32
00:01:24,751 --> 00:01:26,148
It didn't get up again.

33
00:01:26,168 --> 00:01:28,837
With no sun, nothing green grows.

34
00:01:29,117 --> 00:01:32,575
Big animals need huge amounts of food every day.

35
00:01:32,924 --> 00:01:35,640
For a while, the hunters ate the dead.

36
00:01:36,081 --> 00:01:38,183
Then the dead ran out too.

37
00:01:38,718 --> 00:01:41,805
It wasn't only the fire that killed the dinosaurs.

38
00:01:42,153 --> 00:01:44,534
It was the hunger after it.

39
00:01:44,784 --> 00:01:48,510
And the same hunger is now waiting at your door.

40
00:01:49,209 --> 00:01:50,048
Back home,

41
00:01:50,068 --> 00:01:51,716
on your mom's nightstand,

42
00:01:51,844 --> 00:01:53,996
there's a photo: you,

43
00:01:54,016 --> 00:01:55,210
five years old,

44
00:01:55,304 --> 00:01:56,411
on your dad's shoulders,

45
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:58,176
in front of that same T.

46
00:01:58,195 --> 00:01:59,692
rex skeleton.

47
00:02:00,227 --> 00:02:04,465
Your dad helped dig it up, in North Dakota, before you were born.

48
00:02:04,906 --> 00:02:08,016
You haven't let yourself think about him since you got here.

49
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:09,376
Tonight you do.

50
00:02:09,817 --> 00:02:14,078
When you were small, your dad used to put you to bed under his dinosaur posters.

51
00:02:14,612 --> 00:02:17,270
He dug up fossils for a living.

52
00:02:17,721 --> 00:02:21,644
He'd come home with dust in his hair and a story about a bone.

53
00:02:22,179 --> 00:02:23,955
He died when you were seven.

54
00:02:24,304 --> 00:02:25,719
The posters stayed.

55
00:02:25,883 --> 00:02:31,757
And every night, you lay under them and wished, just once, to see a real one.

56
00:02:33,105 --> 00:02:34,382
You got your wish.

57
00:02:34,441 --> 00:02:35,497
You saw them.

58
00:02:35,938 --> 00:02:38,967
And you watched the last of them die.

59
00:02:40,818 --> 00:02:44,962
Back home, sixty-six million years from now, it's day fifty.

60
00:02:45,450 --> 00:02:48,758
On the first morning, your mom knocked to wake you for school.

61
00:02:49,015 --> 00:02:49,943
No answer.

62
00:02:50,338 --> 00:02:52,404
Your door was locked from the inside.

63
00:02:52,602 --> 00:02:54,575
A neighbour had to break it open.

64
00:02:54,969 --> 00:02:56,052
The bed was empty.

65
00:02:56,073 --> 00:02:57,133
The window was shut.

66
00:02:57,153 --> 00:02:59,068
The police still can't explain it.

67
00:02:59,382 --> 00:03:01,753
There's a poster on every lamp post in town.

68
00:03:01,774 --> 00:03:02,760
Your face.

69
00:03:02,796 --> 00:03:03,712
MISSING.

70
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:06,362
Your mom hasn't moved a thing in your room.

71
00:03:06,383 --> 00:03:08,878
Your dad's dinosaur posters.

72
00:03:09,128 --> 00:03:12,019
The charger, still plugged into the wall.

73
00:03:12,519 --> 00:03:16,524
Your two best friends heard it at school, the morning you didn't show up.

74
00:03:17,243 --> 00:03:19,623
The first place they looked was the museum.

75
00:03:19,938 --> 00:03:23,725
When something was wrong, you always went to see the T.

76
00:03:23,746 --> 00:03:24,487
rex.

77
00:03:24,977 --> 00:03:26,973
The one your dad used to take you to.

78
00:03:27,462 --> 00:03:28,439
You weren't there.

79
00:03:28,460 --> 00:03:30,362
They still go back, every week.

80
00:03:30,677 --> 00:03:32,929
They stand where you filmed your video.

81
00:03:33,417 --> 00:03:34,500
"I'd survive.

82
00:03:34,520 --> 00:03:35,320
Easy."

83
00:03:35,426 --> 00:03:37,608
Nobody laughs anymore.

84
00:03:39,456 --> 00:03:42,103
Back in the tunnel, the last fish is gone.

85
00:03:42,418 --> 00:03:43,627
One day with nothing.

86
00:03:43,647 --> 00:03:44,518
Then two.

87
00:03:44,774 --> 00:03:46,224
Your hands start to shake.

88
00:03:46,480 --> 00:03:47,943
Water isn't the problem.

89
00:03:48,338 --> 00:03:50,373
You still dig a pit in the sand by the river,

90
00:03:50,394 --> 00:03:51,081
upstream,

91
00:03:51,102 --> 00:03:52,354
like on day three,

92
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:54,631
and the grey water seeps in clearer.

93
00:03:55,025 --> 00:03:56,058
Fish are.

94
00:03:56,454 --> 00:04:00,830
In this cold, the fish have gone deep, where your hands can't reach.

95
00:04:01,330 --> 00:04:03,210
But Pip isn't starving.

96
00:04:03,803 --> 00:04:08,237
Before the fire, he hid seeds under the roots, the way small animals do.

97
00:04:08,725 --> 00:04:11,383
He's lived on those, and on bugs.

98
00:04:11,779 --> 00:04:13,763
His stash is mouse-sized.

99
00:04:13,904 --> 00:04:16,445
A handful of seeds wouldn't last you an hour.

100
00:04:16,841 --> 00:04:20,555
On day forty-nine, starving, you finally follow him.

101
00:04:21,415 --> 00:04:24,932
He walks past the dead plants and goes straight for a rotten log.

102
00:04:25,247 --> 00:04:26,105
He digs.

103
00:04:26,245 --> 00:04:28,408
And he comes out with a fat white grub.

104
00:04:28,427 --> 00:04:29,379
Then another.

105
00:04:29,634 --> 00:04:31,526
The forest is dead.

106
00:04:31,776 --> 00:04:34,805
But dead things are food, for something.

107
00:04:35,305 --> 00:04:36,825
So you dig next to Pip.

108
00:04:36,861 --> 00:04:37,422
Grubs.

109
00:04:37,442 --> 00:04:38,195
Beetles.

110
00:04:38,394 --> 00:04:41,272
And on the tunnel walls, that soft white fungus.

111
00:04:41,865 --> 00:04:44,046
You don't know which fungus is safe.

112
00:04:44,442 --> 00:04:46,055
Pip sniffs every kind.

113
00:04:46,451 --> 00:04:47,704
He eats the white.

114
00:04:48,192 --> 00:04:49,449
He won't touch the orange.

115
00:04:49,469 --> 00:04:50,920
It's a terrible rule.

116
00:04:51,315 --> 00:04:53,822
Animals can eat things that would kill a person.

117
00:04:53,858 --> 00:04:55,355
But you're out of rules.

118
00:04:55,750 --> 00:04:58,930
You eat one tiny piece of the white, and you wait a whole day.

119
00:04:59,245 --> 00:05:00,567
Nothing happens.

120
00:05:01,056 --> 00:05:03,087
Even that proves nothing.

121
00:05:03,336 --> 00:05:05,971
Some deadly fungi take days to kill you.

122
00:05:06,459 --> 00:05:07,584
You got lucky.

123
00:05:07,899 --> 00:05:09,562
So the white becomes food.

124
00:05:09,582 --> 00:05:12,867
And the orange, you never go near.

125
00:05:13,367 --> 00:05:14,597
Then you get smart.

126
00:05:14,911 --> 00:05:20,239
You drag wet, rotting logs right next to your door, and pile dead leaves on top.

127
00:05:20,728 --> 00:05:22,921
Every few days, you roll one over.

128
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:25,127
There are always grubs underneath.

129
00:05:25,524 --> 00:05:28,714
For the first time since the impact, you have a farm.

130
00:05:29,435 --> 00:05:35,843
Scientists think that's how so many small animals made it: everything that needed fresh plants starved,

131
00:05:36,041 --> 00:05:38,640
but the things that lived on the dead kept going.

132
00:05:39,235 --> 00:05:42,402
Rule four: eat what eats the dead.

133
00:05:42,653 --> 00:05:43,295
Every night,

134
00:05:43,315 --> 00:05:44,503
before you sleep,

135
00:05:44,523 --> 00:05:48,555
you bury the hot coals at the back of the fire under a thick layer of ash,

136
00:05:48,574 --> 00:05:50,605
so they last till morning.

137
00:05:54,156 --> 00:05:56,755
With food in your stomach, you can think again.

138
00:05:57,105 --> 00:06:00,632
And the thought that comes back, every night, is your mom.

139
00:06:01,168 --> 00:06:02,913
She doesn't know where you are.

140
00:06:02,932 --> 00:06:03,935
She never will.

141
00:06:03,954 --> 00:06:05,200
You can't go back.

142
00:06:05,220 --> 00:06:06,762
You've known it for weeks.

143
00:06:07,112 --> 00:06:09,212
Tonight, you say it out loud.

144
00:06:09,655 --> 00:06:11,036
"I can't go back."

145
00:06:11,384 --> 00:06:13,113
Pip looks up at you.

146
00:06:13,613 --> 00:06:16,062
So you stop waiting to wake up.

147
00:06:16,318 --> 00:06:19,127
What's coming is colder and longer than the impact.

148
00:06:19,267 --> 00:06:20,373
You'll need a plan.

149
00:06:20,393 --> 00:06:21,785
Your pen is dry.

150
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:23,868
But the fire leaves charcoal.

151
00:06:23,888 --> 00:06:26,329
You pick up a black stick and write on the tunnel wall,

152
00:06:26,349 --> 00:06:28,717
like you wrote in the book on night one.

153
00:06:29,112 --> 00:06:30,300
What you lost.

154
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:31,526
What you have.

155
00:06:31,667 --> 00:06:33,024
What you still need.

156
00:06:33,420 --> 00:06:35,625
Grubs and fungus keep you alive.

157
00:06:35,661 --> 00:06:37,180
They won't make you strong.

158
00:06:37,495 --> 00:06:40,420
Somewhere out there, there's meat.

159
00:06:43,821 --> 00:06:49,312
Your dad used to say: "Every bone is a message from someone who was here."

160
00:06:50,461 --> 00:06:52,433
Maybe you can't go home.

161
00:06:52,689 --> 00:06:54,307
But maybe a message can.

162
00:06:54,326 --> 00:06:56,879
Almost nothing ever becomes a fossil.

163
00:06:57,194 --> 00:06:59,062
One bone in many millions.

164
00:06:59,319 --> 00:07:03,967
But you know how the lucky ones start: buried fast, deep in mud, like the T.

165
00:07:03,987 --> 00:07:05,197
rex at your door.

166
00:07:05,216 --> 00:07:06,927
And you have a bone.

167
00:07:06,947 --> 00:07:09,663
The one you've been digging with since day four.

168
00:07:10,059 --> 00:07:11,432
It's the only shovel you have.

169
00:07:11,451 --> 00:07:12,879
You'll bury it anyway.

170
00:07:12,985 --> 00:07:16,297
So you take a sharp stone, and you start to write on it.

171
00:07:16,317 --> 00:07:18,288
It takes three nights.

172
00:07:18,738 --> 00:07:20,188
Your fingers bleed.

173
00:07:20,317 --> 00:07:23,509
Your right arm still aches where the claw went in.

174
00:07:23,951 --> 00:07:29,360
You go over every letter again and again, deep enough to last forever.

175
00:07:30,012 --> 00:07:31,072
Three words.

176
00:07:31,091 --> 00:07:36,245
The last two are almost what you said in front of that skeleton, laughing.

177
00:07:36,687 --> 00:07:39,415
Except this time, they're true.

178
00:07:40,356 --> 00:07:41,133
MOM.

179
00:07:41,668 --> 00:07:43,641
I SURVIVED.

180
00:07:44,992 --> 00:07:46,802
Now it has to be buried.

181
00:07:46,838 --> 00:07:47,364
Deep.

182
00:07:47,384 --> 00:07:48,385
In mud.

183
00:07:48,405 --> 00:07:49,831
Where the river floods.

184
00:07:49,973 --> 00:07:52,908
The mud at your door is frozen hard as rock.

185
00:07:52,944 --> 00:07:56,415
The only soft clay left is down at the crocodile's pool.

186
00:07:56,811 --> 00:08:01,547
The river is still running, grey and cold, with ice along the edges in the mornings.

187
00:08:01,943 --> 00:08:04,531
And the river has survivors of its own.

188
00:08:04,845 --> 00:08:05,889
A crocodile.

189
00:08:05,995 --> 00:08:07,944
Maybe the same one from day three.

190
00:08:08,340 --> 00:08:11,659
Crocodiles made it through the end of the world.

191
00:08:11,908 --> 00:08:17,364
And beside it, a champsosaur: a long, thin snout, built for catching fish.

192
00:08:17,853 --> 00:08:19,245
It made it through too.

193
00:08:19,641 --> 00:08:21,392
Crocodiles are cold-blooded.

194
00:08:21,707 --> 00:08:23,819
In this cold, they barely move.

195
00:08:23,925 --> 00:08:26,501
That's how they survived: they almost stop.

196
00:08:27,094 --> 00:08:27,975
Almost.

197
00:08:28,116 --> 00:08:31,365
They're slowest at dawn, before the day warms them.

198
00:08:31,959 --> 00:08:33,653
So you go at first light.

199
00:08:33,909 --> 00:08:37,322
You crawl down the bank with the bone on your back.

200
00:08:37,392 --> 00:08:38,440
At the water's edge,

201
00:08:38,460 --> 00:08:40,386
where the river floods every spring,

202
00:08:40,422 --> 00:08:42,999
you push the bone deep under the clay,

203
00:08:43,243 --> 00:08:45,216
as deep as your arm can reach.

204
00:08:45,786 --> 00:08:47,357
Something moves in the water.

205
00:08:47,377 --> 00:08:48,466
Pip squeaks.

206
00:08:48,653 --> 00:08:49,342
Once.

207
00:08:49,362 --> 00:08:50,149
Loud.

208
00:08:50,221 --> 00:08:52,732
A dark shape drifts toward you, slow as a log.

209
00:08:52,752 --> 00:08:54,183
You throw yourself backwards,

210
00:08:54,203 --> 00:08:56,071
and the crocodile's jaws close,

211
00:08:56,211 --> 00:08:56,876
lazily,

212
00:08:56,896 --> 00:08:59,276
where your hand just was.

213
00:09:00,245 --> 00:09:01,150
Slow.

214
00:09:01,452 --> 00:09:03,322
But not slow enough to be safe.

215
00:09:03,891 --> 00:09:05,794
The bone is under the clay.

216
00:09:06,364 --> 00:09:09,404
You push the last handful over it and run.

217
00:09:10,105 --> 00:09:14,923
Back in the tunnel, you sit with your back against the stone door, shaking.

218
00:09:15,365 --> 00:09:16,339
It's done.

219
00:09:16,688 --> 00:09:20,983
Whatever happens to you now, something of you will last.

220
00:09:21,332 --> 00:09:24,605
But you're tired of always being the one who runs.

221
00:09:25,804 --> 00:09:26,875
That same week,

222
00:09:26,895 --> 00:09:28,903
sixty-six million years later,

223
00:09:28,997 --> 00:09:32,920
your mom goes to the museum for the first time since you disappeared.

224
00:09:33,572 --> 00:09:35,026
She stands in front of the T.

225
00:09:35,046 --> 00:09:36,914
rex for a long time.

226
00:09:37,356 --> 00:09:39,387
An old woman stops next to her.

227
00:09:39,829 --> 00:09:44,298
A grey braid, a sun-faded field hat, dust on her boots.

228
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:47,468
"You're Daniel's wife," she says.

229
00:09:47,689 --> 00:09:49,770
"I dug with him for twelve years.

230
00:09:49,790 --> 00:09:51,810
We found this one together."

231
00:09:52,346 --> 00:09:54,329
She looks at the floor.

232
00:09:54,779 --> 00:09:57,476
"I've seen your boy's face on every lamp post.

233
00:09:57,496 --> 00:09:58,923
I'm so sorry."

234
00:09:59,562 --> 00:10:02,104
They talk until the museum closes.

235
00:10:02,871 --> 00:10:03,641
At the door,

236
00:10:03,661 --> 00:10:07,642
the old woman says she's going back to his old dig site in North Dakota.

237
00:10:08,072 --> 00:10:09,151
Next week.

238
00:10:09,199 --> 00:10:11,276
The last time, at her age.

239
00:10:11,812 --> 00:10:16,477
"He always said that place had one more surprise in it."

240
00:10:17,827 --> 00:10:19,544
The cold is getting worse.

241
00:10:19,928 --> 00:10:21,995
One night, you wake up coughing.

242
00:10:22,564 --> 00:10:24,989
Snow has piled against the stone door.

243
00:10:25,177 --> 00:10:27,300
The fire's heat melted the bottom of it,

244
00:10:27,441 --> 00:10:29,726
and the meltwater ran down through the gap,

245
00:10:29,762 --> 00:10:31,015
right onto the fire.

246
00:10:31,399 --> 00:10:34,184
Then the top froze over and sealed the gap shut.

247
00:10:34,429 --> 00:10:35,961
The fire is drowned.

248
00:10:36,148 --> 00:10:38,712
The smoke from the wet wood has nowhere to go.

249
00:10:39,177 --> 00:10:41,928
It's rolling back into the tunnel, low and grey.

250
00:10:42,173 --> 00:10:45,283
Smoke in a closed tunnel kills quietly.

251
00:10:45,355 --> 00:10:47,350
You've known that since the first fire.

252
00:10:47,596 --> 00:10:49,014
You crawl to the door,

253
00:10:49,034 --> 00:10:51,727
punch through the ice in the gap with your bare hands,

254
00:10:51,764 --> 00:10:52,750
and breathe.

255
00:10:53,134 --> 00:10:54,124
No matches.

256
00:10:54,144 --> 00:10:55,014
No lighter.

257
00:10:55,479 --> 00:10:59,471
You try a bow drill, the way you've seen in videos, until your hands blister.

258
00:10:59,716 --> 00:11:01,066
The wood is too damp.

259
00:11:01,086 --> 00:11:01,793
Nothing.

260
00:11:02,259 --> 00:11:05,961
There's one chance left: the coals you buried at the back, under the ash.

261
00:11:06,345 --> 00:11:08,369
You dig into the grey pile with a stick.

262
00:11:08,389 --> 00:11:08,822
Wet.

263
00:11:08,841 --> 00:11:09,321
Black.

264
00:11:09,341 --> 00:11:10,581
Black.

265
00:11:10,653 --> 00:11:14,263
Then, at the very bottom, one tiny red eye.

266
00:11:14,728 --> 00:11:17,187
You lay a pinch of dry fern fluff on it.

267
00:11:17,432 --> 00:11:18,418
You blow.

268
00:11:18,792 --> 00:11:19,573
Gently.

269
00:11:19,593 --> 00:11:20,438
Again.

270
00:11:20,742 --> 00:11:21,848
A thread of smoke.

271
00:11:21,868 --> 00:11:22,958
Then a flame.

272
00:11:23,331 --> 00:11:24,310
From that night on,

273
00:11:24,330 --> 00:11:26,040
you sleep with your head at the door,

274
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:28,961
and you check the gap every time you wake.

275
00:11:29,461 --> 00:11:32,187
One morning, the sand pit is frozen solid.

276
00:11:32,387 --> 00:11:34,801
So you work down the list on the wall.

277
00:11:35,115 --> 00:11:39,538
You dry grubs hard on a flat stone by the fire, the way you dried fish.

278
00:11:39,793 --> 00:11:41,290
They'll keep for weeks.

279
00:11:41,605 --> 00:11:42,979
You bring snow inside,

280
00:11:42,999 --> 00:11:44,360
melt it by the fire,

281
00:11:44,379 --> 00:11:46,555
and pour it through sand and charcoal,

282
00:11:46,575 --> 00:11:48,779
because this snow is full of ash.

283
00:11:49,174 --> 00:11:50,033
One tick.

284
00:11:50,522 --> 00:11:51,588
Then another.

285
00:11:51,846 --> 00:11:54,701
But two lines on the wall still have question marks.

286
00:11:55,293 --> 00:11:58,125
Your tunnel was built to survive the impact.

287
00:11:58,375 --> 00:11:59,511
Not a winter.

288
00:11:59,768 --> 00:12:01,346
You'll need somewhere deeper.

289
00:12:01,381 --> 00:12:02,228
Warmer.

290
00:12:02,543 --> 00:12:04,677
But that's a problem for another day.

291
00:12:05,270 --> 00:12:07,231
The other question mark can't wait.

292
00:12:07,721 --> 00:12:08,432
That night,

293
00:12:08,451 --> 00:12:11,376
you pick up the sharp stone you carved the bone with,

294
00:12:11,447 --> 00:12:12,053
and a long,

295
00:12:12,073 --> 00:12:13,083
straight branch,

296
00:12:13,119 --> 00:12:14,812
and you start to make a spear.

297
00:12:14,884 --> 00:12:16,384
Tomorrow, you stop hiding.

298
00:12:16,404 --> 00:12:18,261
You learn to hunt.

299
00:12:19,611 --> 00:12:22,559
A few days later, Pip doesn't come back at night.

300
00:12:22,722 --> 00:12:23,550
You wait.

301
00:12:23,570 --> 00:12:24,223
One night.

302
00:12:24,243 --> 00:12:25,566
Two.

303
00:12:26,317 --> 00:12:28,521
On the third morning, he's at the entrance.

304
00:12:28,662 --> 00:12:29,937
And he's not alone.

305
00:12:30,195 --> 00:12:31,691
A second little face.

306
00:12:31,797 --> 00:12:32,585
Smaller.

307
00:12:32,899 --> 00:12:36,741
Pale, ash-grey fur, with a white mark on her chest.

308
00:12:37,230 --> 00:12:38,831
The same black eyes.

309
00:12:39,226 --> 00:12:43,649
She survived the dark too, in some other hole, under some other tree.

310
00:12:44,241 --> 00:12:46,992
Pip isn't the last of his kind.

311
00:12:48,342 --> 00:12:49,529
You're happy for him.

312
00:12:49,549 --> 00:12:50,733
You really are.

313
00:12:51,175 --> 00:12:54,115
But that night, the tunnel feels bigger than it ever has.

314
00:12:54,135 --> 00:12:56,247
You scratch a new line on the wall.

315
00:12:56,783 --> 00:12:58,434
Friends: one.

316
00:12:58,454 --> 00:12:59,510
You stop.

317
00:12:59,789 --> 00:13:01,326
And you add: …

318
00:13:01,346 --> 00:13:03,260
and one more.

319
00:13:05,110 --> 00:13:06,341
North Dakota.

320
00:13:06,538 --> 00:13:08,307
Your dad's old dig site.

321
00:13:08,326 --> 00:13:10,020
A cold, bright afternoon.

322
00:13:10,057 --> 00:13:11,738
The last dig of the season.

323
00:13:11,879 --> 00:13:18,043
The old woman from the museum kneels in the dirt and brushes the dust off the shoulder blade of a duck-billed dinosaur.

324
00:13:18,357 --> 00:13:19,238
She stops.

325
00:13:19,727 --> 00:13:21,328
There are marks on the bone.

326
00:13:21,365 --> 00:13:22,675
Not teeth marks.

327
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:23,841
Too straight.

328
00:13:23,860 --> 00:13:24,754
Too deep.

329
00:13:25,067 --> 00:13:26,309
She brushes again.

330
00:13:26,798 --> 00:13:28,032
They're not scratches.

331
00:13:28,052 --> 00:13:30,059
They're letters.

332
00:13:31,210 --> 00:13:35,017
If you could leave one message for 66 million years…

333
00:13:35,506 --> 00:13:37,258
what would you write on the bone?

334
00:13:37,850 --> 00:13:39,394
Tell me in the comments.

335
00:13:41,512 --> 00:13:42,442
Day seventy.

336
00:13:42,932 --> 00:13:44,262
The river is frozen.

337
00:13:44,472 --> 00:13:46,382
Under the ice, there is food…

338
00:13:46,492 --> 00:13:47,862
and something that breathes.

339
00:13:48,472 --> 00:13:49,722
Water bends light.

340
00:13:50,312 --> 00:13:52,582
The spear jerks in your hands.

341
00:13:53,672 --> 00:13:54,942
It doesn't even crack.

342
00:13:56,272 --> 00:13:58,202
She wakes up with her heart pounding.

343
00:13:58,392 --> 00:13:59,762
"That's his handwriting."

344
00:14:00,432 --> 00:14:02,422
GONE EAST. DAY 88.

345
00:14:02,752 --> 00:14:04,142
…and follow it east.

346
00:14:08,394 --> 00:14:09,152
Stay small.

347
00:14:09,172 --> 00:14:10,308
Stay alive.

